\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Pinning and mean zero on two vertices}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{Join vertices #{0,1} by one edge of weight #{w>0}. In counting
coordinates its Laplacian is
##{L=w\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\-1&1\end{pmatrix}.}
The unit mean-zero vector #{u=(1,-1)/\sqrt2} has eigenvalue #{2w}.
Thus the [mean-zero law](fdip-000C) has
##{\begin{aligned}
Z_H&=\sqrt{2\pi}/\sqrt{2w},\\
L^+&=\frac1{4w}\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\-1&1\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
The factor #{1/\sqrt2} is required because the reference measure is the
induced orthonormal volume on #{H}.}
\p{Pinning #{\phi_0=0} leaves the unit vector #{\delta_1}; its action
is #{w\phi_1^2/2}. The [pinned law](fdip-0035) therefore has
##{\begin{aligned}
Z_F&=\sqrt{2\pi}/\sqrt w,\\
C_F&=\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\0&1/w\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
These ambient coordinate covariances differ. But for the difference
#{\phi_0-\phi_1}, applying the two covariance forms to #{(1,-1)} gives
#{1/w} in both cases. This agreement of one observable does not identify
the laws. For a single vertex the zero-pinned and mean-zero spaces are
both zero, so that special case has no such distinction.}
\p{These are direct one-dimensional Gaussian calculations using the
positive density in \citet{Proposition B.55, p. 505}{friedli2017statistical}
and the two explicitly specified induced volumes.}
